# Target C: loaded-neighbor incidence sharpens outer-owner blocker capacity

Date: 2026-10-05. Base main: `0351c2c3aecd34c0427d0ad081afae563468d401`.
State: `2026-10-05.audit.61`.
Classification: **proved candidate awaiting independent audit**; artifact
lifecycle **unreviewed**. C-II and Level I remain open.

## 1. Exact implication

```
actual O--O owners with certified upper and unary-rhombus safety
+ each singleton shortens an old diameter
-> every owner needs an outer-face near-unit witness
-> loaded incidence limits charge witnesses to nonunit hull sides,
   unloaded outer core points and outer-face isolates
-> k<=2m_B+3(x_B+Z_out)
-> a linear owner family is excluded if those three banks are sublinear.
```

This supplies a conditional contradiction, not an extraction of that
owner family or a proof that every bank is sublinear. The only source
geometry used is canonical full-source midpoint concentration from
Section 58 and strict support at actual diameter endpoints. The owner
application uses canonical audit.61's actual O--O localization, including
all its cloud, ownership, isolate-dispatch and unary-rhombus hypotheses.

## 2. Every source point has at most two nearby loaded neighbors

Fix any t with 1<t<sqrt(2). For every sufficiently large canonical source,
every source vertex y has at most two distinct actual loaded vertices
p!=y with |p-y|<=t. The threshold depends on t, not on the source or its
isolate population.

**Proof.** Otherwise take sources with D tending to infinity, a witness y,
and three such loaded points. Translate y to the origin. Their distances
are in [1,t] by source unit separation, and their mutual distances are
at least one. For each choose an actual diameter normal n_i and midpoint
m_i. Canonical |m_i-o|<=T with T=O(D^(3/4)) gives

    D(n_i-n_j)=2(p_i-p_j)-2(m_i-m_j),
    sin(theta_ij/2)<=(2t+2T)/D=o(1).

All three normals thus lie in an angular interval of width alpha=o(1).
Each normal strictly supports the old source at its loaded point:

    n_i dot(w-p_i)<=-|w-p_i|^2/(2D)<0.

Order the points by these normals. The chords p_2-p_1 and p_3-p_2 have
directions between the corresponding quarter-turned normals; their
forward directions differ by at most alpha. Passing to a subsequence
inside the compact annulus, the three distinct limit points are collinear
in that order, with successive separations at least one.

Three unit-separated collinear points cannot all lie in the annulus
1<=|p|<=t when t<sqrt(2). If their line is at distance h>=1 from the
origin, its outer-disk chord has length at most 2sqrt(t^2-1)<2. If h<1,
the allowed line points lie in two intervals, each of length

    sqrt(t^2-h^2)-sqrt(1-h^2)
    <=sqrt(t^2-1)<1.

There is at most one separated point in each interval. Both cases
contradict the three limit points. This proves the uniform asymptotic
statement. QED.

The argument includes source isolates as y and asserts no equilibrium
at them. It does not identify every loaded neighbor with a contact.

## 3. Nearby loaded-loaded pairs are consecutive hull pairs

For the same fixed t<sqrt(2) and large D, any two distinct loaded vertices
p,q with |p-q|<=t are consecutive vertices of the full source hull.

Their chosen actual diameter normals are at angle theta=o(1), by the
same midpoint identity. The hull chain between the corresponding strict
support vertices on that short normal interval has side directions within
theta of one common tangent. Every hull side joins distinct source points
and has length at least one. If there were an intermediate hull vertex,
projection onto the bisector tangent would give

    |p-q|>=2cos(theta/2)=2-o(1)>t,

a contradiction. Hence they are consecutive in the full hull and therefore
also consecutive in the hull of the loaded vertices.

Let m_B be the number of nonunit consecutive sides in the loaded force
hull. Every loaded-loaded **noncontact** pair of length at most t is one
of these m_B sides. This uses no extension of the full-core loaded-contact
chord theorem to the container branch: it is a direct consequence of
actual diameter support normals in the full source.

## 4. Outer-owner capacity theorem

Take an actual disjoint-owner finite move family satisfying the promoted
O--O hypotheses. Reassign bounded-face isolates as in audit.61. Assume:

- every old contact stays unit for every binary selection;
- every singleton keeps old diameters at most D and old nondiameters
  strictly below D;
- every unary strict-rhombus diagonal remains strict for each singleton;
- every nonzero singleton owner proposal shortens an old diameter;
- every possible lower witness has old length at most the fixed t above.

The last assumption follows, for example, from a displacement bound
1+2s<t. Let k be the number of surviving owners. Let U_out be the distinct
unloaded core points on the outer contact face, and Z_out the contact
isolates in that face. Put W=U_out union Z_out.

Every singleton must fail at an outer-face old noncontact pair, by
canonical normalization and audit.61 localization. Choose one witness
and its relatively moving endpoint for each owner. Classify these choices:

1. Both endpoints are loaded. By Section 3 there are at most m_B possible
   pairs, and a pair can witness at most its two endpoint owners. These
   choices account for at most 2m_B owners.
2. The moving endpoint is in W. Owners are disjoint, so such a vertex
   belongs to only one owner. These choices account for at most |W| owners.
3. The moving endpoint is loaded and the other endpoint is in W. By
   Section 2 each W vertex has at most two nearby loaded vertices, each
   belonging to at most one owner. These choices account for at most
   2|W| owners.

These cases exhaust the choices and prove

    k<=2m_B+3(|U_out|+Z_out).                         (4.1)

For the connected core outer walk of length b, every one of its q loaded
vertices occurs at least once. Therefore |U_out|<=b-q=x_B, and

    k<=2m_B+3(x_B+Z_out).                            (4.2)

Repeated outer-walk occurrences are retained in b; they are not identified
with distinct source vertices. An owner is charged at an actual moved
endpoint, not merely to an occurrence or a contracted source path.

Where the canonical finite-compatibility deletion of at most 2d_H owners
applies to an original r-proposal family, with the remaining hypotheses
verified after deletion, the same proof gives

    r<=2m_B+3(x_B+Z_out)+2d_H.                       (4.3)

This does not suppress upper-induced propagation: deletion and the
remaining singleton hypotheses must be checked as in the canonical
finite proposal theorem, rather than inferred from an arbitrary Horn
formula.

## 5. Composition and live obstruction

Equation (4.2) improves the canonical conditional bound k<=b+Z_out by
removing q from the capacity bank. In particular an actual k=Omega(D)
family is impossible if m_B,x_B,Z_out=o(D). On scopes where the promoted
hull-side ledger gives m_B<=x_B, the bound becomes k<=5x_B+3Z_out.
Thus with x_B=o(D), a linear safe singleton family forces a linear
outer-face isolate population.

This does not prove that such a family is supplied in every branch.
The physical pentagon-pair train bank is not automatically an O--O
owner bank. Nor does Type II alone make Z_out=o(D); a linear isolate
population remains possible. Unary rhombus failures and upper exceptions
are not charged to m_B or W when their safety hypotheses fail. The
macroscopic-container branch likewise needs its own actual move extraction.

Hence the next obstruction in the applicable low-defect case is explicit:
a linear outer-isolate bank, absence of enough usable O--O proposals,
or a failed unary/upper hypothesis. No exhaustive C-II branch is closed.

## 6. Validation

The proof is analytic midpoint geometry, compactness in a fixed annulus
and exact finite endpoint counting. No numerical verifier is needed or
claimed. The sufficient-large-D quantifier is justified by the uniform
canonical T/D bound and the contradiction subsequence, not a numerical
threshold. Repository checks certify state/index consistency only.
